Good procedures are written down, easy to find, easy to read, and tailored to what the firm actually does. They typically:

- name a **compliance officer** with clearly defined authority, resources and the power to investigate;
- set out who supervises whom and how duties are divided;
- build in **checks and balances**;
- state their scope and what conduct is allowed;
- explain how violations are reported and what sanctions follow;
- document how the procedures themselves are monitored and tested.

After adoption, the supervisor spreads the program to staff, updates it regularly, reminds and trains people, reviews what employees do, and enforces the rules. Including a conduct assessment in performance reviews and rewarding ethical behavior (not only sales) helps the program work.
